// A @none operator is not merely unresolved, it is rejected. Where @left and @right turn a shift/reduce conflict into a
// shift or a reduction, @none turns it into an error action which is written into the table like any other action, so
// `1 < 2 < 3` is a syntax error while `1 < 2` is not. That error action is the point of the case: it is the one way a
// state can refuse a terminal which is otherwise perfectly well known to it, and a backend which drops such entries
// while compressing the table accepts a sentence the grammar rejects.
//
// The grammar marks no place to resume at, so the recovery unwinds the whole stack in vain and the parse is over. The
// offset the error is reported at is the second "<", which is the one the parser refused.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are a single character and runs of digits.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    NUMBER: /[0-9]+/;
    LESS:   "<";
}

@parser {
    @precedence {
        @none: "<";
    }

    comparison
        : NUMBER
        | comparison "<" comparison
        ;
}
